    {
      "id": "chatcmpl-E52zRktxycKt1pw605fmSG0yhUdUy",
      "role": "assistant",
      "created": 1784871842,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I couldn’t compute the ranking because the mandatory MedCP clinical database connection failed:\n\n`Adaptive Server connection failed (QCDIDDWDB001.ucsfmedicalcenter.org)`\n\nIf the connection is restored, I would rank acid-suppressing medications/classes—especially PPIs and H2 blockers—by the count or rate of patients with a subsequent C. difficile diagnosis code within 5 years of first exposure. Relevant drug groups would include:\n\n- Proton pump inhibitors: omeprazole, pantoprazole, lansoprazole, esomeprazole, rabeprazole, dexlansoprazole\n- H2 receptor antagonists: famotidine, ranitidine, cimetidine, nizatidine\n- Other acid-reducing/antacid agents depending on medication table coverage\n\nC. difficile codes would typically include ICD-10 `A04.7`, `A04.71`, `A04.72`, and ICD-9 `008.45`.\n\nI can rerun the MedCP query once the database is reachable."
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "userVisible": true,
        "agentVisible": true
      }
